#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087689 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087494 :question/id #uuid "5e22ac55-3e84-5cb4-8231-5f882da9240e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4103" :question/problem "What is the value of $x$ if $e^{x - 3}=2^5?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-3} & ={2}^{5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\ln \\left( e^a \\right) = a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx-3 = \\ln \\left( 2^5 \\right).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$ and rewrite the right-hand side using the power law of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5}) \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5})+3 \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=5\\ln (2) + 3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d8c6c93-9d15-5482-8130-25bb9cabf008" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049053 :topic/id #uuid "d5efe33b-5861-5f40-bd4b-3f871de6b84d" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110593} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110595}] :topic/math-academy-id 1482 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049643} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050649}] :topic/title "Solving Exponential Equations Using Logarithms"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049589 :topic/id #uuid "02b0bd28-fe3c-5e27-b64f-dcec4176a6ea" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087692}] :topic/math-academy-id 818 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049560} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050312}